I first ran into the level-0 troll phenomenon in regular game play, not 
in wizard mode. I used wizard mode in the bug report to simplify bug 
reproduction.

The principle reason I reported this as a bug: I was concerned that 
having a character drain to level 0 was a symptom of some larger 
level-tracking bug. Unlike Dylan and others, I don't know the source -- 
I assess the game as a user.

I worked in Microsoft developer support during the Windows 3.0 beta in 
1989 and 1990. Our team collectively took thousands of bug reports from 
ISVs beta testing Windows. We all agreed we'd rather have too many bug 
reports than too few, even if that meant a large number of "won't fix" 
or "not a bug" replies to the beta testers.

Because NetHack is in continual development -- and indeed the manual 
page claims the number of bugs is probably infinite -- I consider 
myself a beta tester as well as a player. If I see something that seems 
inconsistent or wrong, I err on the side of telling the development 
team. I also trust that the development team -- the only people who 
really define what is and isn't intended behavior -- can handle a filed 
bug report for something they don't consider a bug.

And if this helps, Kevin: I have submitted several bug reports to the 
development team that they responded to within 24 hours, telling me 
that the bugs were real and that they'd fixed them after my report.

